; ispconfig-sso — optional configuration override.
; Copyright (c) 2026 Wade Beckett. GPLv3 — see ../LICENSE.
;
; ============================================================================
; THIS FILE IS AN EXAMPLE. Copy it into place; do not edit it here.
;
;   cp conf/connectsso.ini.example /usr/local/ispconfig/interface/lib/connectsso.ini
;   chown ispconfig:ispconfig      /usr/local/ispconfig/interface/lib/connectsso.ini
;   chmod 640                      /usr/local/ispconfig/interface/lib/connectsso.ini
;
; Check what the panel actually ended up running on:
;
;   ./install.sh --status /usr/local/ispconfig
; ============================================================================
;
; WHY THE FILE LIVES THERE, above the panel's document root:
;
;   - It cannot be fetched over HTTP. The panel vhost roots at
;     <ispconfig>/interface/web/ (install/tpl/apache_ispconfig.vhost.master
;     DocumentRoot, install/tpl/nginx_ispconfig.vhost.master root).
;     interface/lib/ is the directory above that, where ISPConfig keeps its own
;     config.inc.php. No request can reach it.
;   - ./install.sh never writes it and ./uninstall.sh never deletes it. The
;     installer only ever creates and removes interface/web/connectsso; your
;     configuration is not the installer's to destroy. The uninstaller prints
;     the path and leaves the file where it is.
;   - An ISPConfig upgrade leaves it alone. install_ispconfig() deploys the
;     interface with `cp -rf ../interface <install_dir>`
;     (install/lib/installer_base.lib.php:3599) — a merge, not a replace, which
;     is the same reason the module directory itself survives — and then
;     re-runs `chown -R ispconfig:ispconfig <install_dir>/interface` (:3774), so
;     the panel can still read it afterwards.
;
; This is NOT security_settings.ini, which would have been the obvious home:
; the ISPConfig installer rewrites that file from a master template on every
; install and upgrade (installer_base.lib.php:3613-3618), so a setting placed
; there would be silently lost — the exact failure this file exists to prevent.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT YOU CAN CHANGE — the whole list is below. Every setting is OPTIONAL:
; delete a line and its shipped default applies. Every value is validated and
; CLAMPED to the range in its comment; a value outside the range is corrected,
; not obeyed, and the correction is written to Monitor > System Log and shown
; by ./install.sh --status. A key this file does not recognise is ignored and
; reported — a typo never silently does nothing.
;
; A file that cannot be parsed leaves EVERY shipped default in force and is
; complained about loudly. Single sign-on keeps working; it just ignores you.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

; How long a minted token stays redeemable, in seconds.
;
; This is a hand-off window, not a session: the only thing that happens between
; minting and redeeming is one HTTP redirect. Longer widens the window in which
; a token scraped from a web-server access log or a browser history entry is
; still live. Raise it only if your customers genuinely fail to complete the
; redirect in time.
;
; Range 5-60. Default 45.
;token_ttl = 45

; Rate limit on the redemption endpoint: failed attempts allowed from one source
; address inside the window below, before it is refused with 429. This is the
; same budget ISPConfig's own remote API applies (remoting.inc.php:94).
;
; Only refusals reached by presenting a token value that did not check out are
; counted — see the README's security model. Note that behind a proxy which
; does not set REMOTE_ADDR, every visitor shares one bucket, which is a reason
; to raise this rather than lower it.
;
; Range 1-1000. Default 10. (0 is not accepted: it would refuse everyone,
; forever, and is corrected to 1.)
;attempt_limit = 10

; The window those attempts are counted in, in seconds.
;
; Range 60-86400. Default 300.
;attempt_window = 300

; May a token be minted with no bind_ip, i.e. redeemable from any address?
;
; Turn this off ONLY if you are sure the panel and your billing system see the
; same client address. When they do not — a CDN or reverse proxy in front of
; either, a proxy that does not set REMOTE_ADDR, an IPv4/IPv6 asymmetry — every
; single sign-on attempt fails, because the caller is then forced to supply a
; bind IP that will never match. With it off, a mint that passes an empty
; bind_ip is refused with:
;
;   This panel requires single sign-on tokens to be bound to an IP address.
;
; yes or no. Default yes.
;allow_unbound_tokens = yes

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT YOU CANNOT CHANGE, and why it is not an oversight
;
; A token is a password substitute. The settings above tune how long it lives
; and how hard it is to guess at; the controls below are what make a leaked one
; worth almost nothing, and none of them is a knob:
;
;   token entropy      32 bytes (256 bits) from random_bytes(), always.
;   hashing at rest    SHA-256, always. The clear token is never stored and
;                      never logged, so reading the table yields no login.
;   single use         the first redemption burns it, always. The guard is a
;                      conditional UPDATE and its affected-row count.
;   administrators     sys_user.typ = 'admin' is refused at mint AND again at
;                      redemption, always. There is no flag for this.
;   TLS                both halves refuse a plaintext request. If a reverse
;                      proxy terminates TLS for you, declare THAT — in
;                      ISPConfig's own security_settings.ini, [permissions],
;                      reverse_proxy_panel_allowed. It is not a setting here.
;
; Keys naming any of them are refused BY NAME, with the reason, so nobody is
; left believing they turned one off. In full, the refused names are:
;
;   token_bytes, token_entropy   token_hash, hash_algo
;   single_use,  allow_reuse     allow_admin
;   require_tls, allow_http
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
